<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Juan Negrín</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1892">1892</a>–<a href="/years/1956">1956</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Juan Negrín was a Spanish physiologist and Socialist politician who served as prime minister of the Second Republic from May 1937 to the end of the Civil War. He centralised the war effort, relied on Soviet aid and argued for resistance in the hope that a wider European war would save the Republic. He led the government in exile until 1945 and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/juan-negrin.jpg" alt="Juan Negrín" width="279" height="357" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Juan_Negr%C3%ADn_en_Barcelona_1938.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Juan Negrín was born on 3 February 1892 in Las Palmas in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canary-islands">Canary Islands</a>, into a deeply Catholic middle-class family; his father was a prosperous merchant. Having shown interest in medicine, he was sent at 15 to study in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiel">Kiel</a> and then at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>. In 1912, aged twenty, he took a doctorate in medicine and became an assistant at the Institute of Physiology in Leipzig.</p><p>Returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> at the end of 1915, he became a professor of physiology at the Complutense University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> in 1922, and his laboratory became an internationally known research centre. Among his students was Severo Ochoa, later a Nobel laureate. Close to German social democracy, he joined the Spanish Socialist Workers' Party (PSOE) in 1929, sided with the moderate faction of Indalecio Prieto, and was elected deputy for Las Palmas in 1931, 1933 and 1936.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the military uprising of 17 July 1936 he helped many people escape the revolutionary checas in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> and was nearly killed by anarchists. In September 1936 he became minister of finance under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-largo-caballero">Francisco Largo Caballero</a>. In October 1936, with the approval of President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-azana">Manuel Azaña</a> and other ministers, he sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>'s gold reserves to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in return for arms; critics argued that this put the government under Stalin's control.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona-may-days">Barcelona May Days</a> forced Largo Caballero to resign, Azaña named Negrín prime minister on 17 May 1937. His cabinet included Prieto at War, the communists Jesús Hernández Tomás and Vicente Uribe, and the republican José Giral. Under the slogan 'Resistir es vencer', 'To resist is to win', he sought to keep the Republic alive until a wider war brought it allies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a>. He remained in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> until the Republican front collapsed in March 1939.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Negrín lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> until the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in June 1940 and then in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, heading the Republican government in exile until August 1945, when he resigned and José Giral succeeded him. Franco's courts stripped him of his chair and fined him 100 million pesetas. The PSOE expelled him in April 1946 and rehabilitated him in 2008. He died of a heart attack in Paris on 12 November 1956, aged 64. Historians differ about him: later scholarship has portrayed him as a pragmatic social democrat who had no choice but to ally with the Soviets, because <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and France would not support the Republic.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Juan Negrín” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>During the war the Republic had three governments, led by José Giral, Francisco Largo Caballero and <b>Juan Negrín</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/second-spanish-republic">Second Spanish Republic · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>After the May Days of 1937 weakened the anarchists, the <b>Negrín</b> government dissolved the council in August 1937.</blockquote><a href="/state/regional-defence-council-of-aragon">Regional Defence Council of Aragon</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1933" aria-label="Paris on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Juan Negrín"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5132 5945h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5132 5945h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1933">What was here in 1933? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
